            'Evidence for young rings: (1) bright, nearly uncontaminated water ice — ancient rings would be much darker from continuous bombardment by interplanetary dust (micrometeorites) which would mix dark organic and silicate material into the ice; the rings\' current high albedo implies they have not had 4.5 billion years of micrometeorite darkening; (2) the ring-rain mass loss rate of ~10,000 kg/s measured directly by Cassini implies the rings would vanish in 100–300 million years at current rates; implication: the rings formed relatively recently from a comet, moon, or Kuiper Belt object that strayed within the Roche limit and was tidally disrupted; Saturn was ringless for most of its 4.5-billion-year history and will be ringless again within the next few hundred million years',
