            'Evidence for young rings: the ring particles are chemically identical to Saturn\'s moon Enceladus in composition, proving the rings formed from material ejected by Enceladus\'s geysers over the last few hundred million years; ancient ring material would have been processed by radiation into complex organic compounds not found in the rings; implication: the rings are a direct product of Enceladus\'s cryovolcanism and will continue to exist as long as Enceladus remains active; the E ring and main rings are part of the same connected system',
            'Evidence for young rings: the rings orbit Saturn at the exact Keplerian velocity for their current radius, proving they formed in place during Saturn\'s formation 4.5 billion years ago; if the rings were younger and formed from a tidally disrupted body, they would have residual non-Keplerian velocities; the confusion about ring age arose from early estimates that incorrectly assumed all ring material must have the same age; Cassini showed that while the ring structure is ancient, individual ring particles are replaced every ~10,000 years by meteorite recycling',
